1: # The full Euler cube for the actual source shear
2: 
3: Proved as P3K.66 after full root and independent blind_control
4: audits, 17 November 2026. The exact coefficient and sign checks
5: are also executed in the saved certificate.
6: We retain the selected-cusp positive rho loop, its actual source and
7: finite caps, the physical delta action, and the fans
8: \(T_n=T(e_1+ne_2,e_2)\), for every integer \(n\). All quotient-piece,
9: source, normal and cap maps used below are the literal maps of
10: P3K.63. We do not replace any of these maps by a comparison of their
11: homology groups.
12: 
13: ## EC.1. The real connection, the actual complex line, and orientation
14: 
15: We first fix the signs of a real circle connection explicitly. Let
16: \(\mathcal L_\delta\) be the complex line whose nonzero vectors are the
17: actual physical principal circle bundle. Its local fibre coefficient
18: is \(V=|V|\exp(2\pi i d)\); the physical delta action increases \(d\).
19: A real principal connection \(A\), normalized by
20: \(A(\partial_d)=1\), gives the unitary line connection \(2\pi i A\)
21: in local unit frames. Consequently
22: \[
23:  c_1(\mathcal L_\delta)
24:        =\frac{i}{2\pi}F_{\nabla_\delta}=-dA.
25:                                                         \tag{EC.1}
26: \]
27: For example, in the actual Cox coordinate
28: \(q_H=(1+jZ)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\), the physical action is
29: \(q_H\mapsto q_H e^{-2\pi i t}\). Its real connection is minus the
30: usual contact connection in that right-complex spinor. Formula
31: (EC.1) gives the positive meridian class retained in F56 and K42.
32: It is the same complex line as the source line on a regular source
33: slice: K6's literal page restriction is
34: \(x|_{\mathrm{page}}=c_1(M)\). We retain this arrow, rather than change
35: the associated representation of the circle.
36: 
37: It is convenient to perform the connection calculation on the inverse
38: line
39: \[
40:  \mathcal U=\mathcal L_\delta^{-1},\qquad
41:  \widehat x=c_1(\mathcal U)=-x=dA.
42:                                                         \tag{EC.2}
43: \]
44: Thus the real forms in SQ.12--15 represent \(\widehat x\) when the
45: principal coordinate is the increasing physical delta coefficient
46: \(d\). This makes the associated-line sign explicit. The vanishing
47: Pontryagin pairing of K63 is unaffected by replacing \(x\) by \(-x\);
48: its statement and relative-coordinate conclusions are unchanged.
49: 
50: We also keep the contracting suspension coefficient
51: \(\gamma=\gamma_B\). In physical coordinates
52: \(V=r\exp(2\pi i d)\), the complex threefold has orientation
53: \(\Omega_S\wedge dr\wedge dd\). Taking the physical delta circle
54: first gives orbit orientation
55: \(-\Omega_S\wedge d\log r\). The contracting coefficient increases
56: as \(\log r\) decreases. Hence its orbit orientation is
57: \(\Omega_S\wedge d\gamma_B\), as calculated in GO.1. The physical
58: positive-parameter completion therefore uses
59: \(\Omega_S\wedge d\gamma_B\wedge d\theta\). K42's actual parameter
60: reversal to the Jin completion reverses this orientation and carries
61: the same line \(\mathcal L_\delta\).
62: 
63: Write \(r_q\) for an increasing radial coordinate in the q disc and
64: \(\alpha=\arg(q)/(2\pi)\). The positive source orientation near its
65: regular boundary is
66: \[
67:  dr_q\wedge d\alpha\wedge du\wedge dv .
68:                                                         \tag{EC.3}
69: \]
70: Indeed the source fibre is \(u+\tau v\), with
71: \(\operatorname{Im}\tau>0\). The two reversals, of the Jin
72: orientation and of the odd cube \(x^3=(-\widehat x)^3\), cancel.
73: We can therefore compute the actual Jin integer \(k\) by integrating
74: the cube of the curvature of \(\mathcal U\) in the auxiliary
75: orientation
76: \[
77:  \Omega_+=dr_q\wedge d\alpha\wedge du\wedge dv
78:                          \wedge d\gamma_B\wedge d\theta .
79:                                                         \tag{EC.4}
80: \]
81: This is an equality of the specified oriented integrals. No integer
82: lift of a residue is used to choose its sign.
83: 
84: ## EC.2. Full periods and exact boundary line coordinates
85: 
86: Retain SQ.1--2 in full:
87: \[
88:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(\kappa qZ,\alpha_L V),\qquad
89:  B:(Z,V)\mapsto(gZ,\eta qV),
90: \]
91: \[
92:  Z_n=Z,\quad V_n=Z^{-n}V,\quad h_n=hB^n,\quad B_n=B,
93: \]
94: \[
95:  Q_n=\kappa qg^n,\quad
96:  \alpha_{L,n}=\alpha_L\eta^n\kappa^{-n}g^{-n^2},
97:  \qquad\eta_n=\eta g^{-n}.
98:                                                         \tag{EC.5}
99: \]
100: The symbol \(\alpha_L\) here denotes the line multiplier; it is
101: not the angle \(\alpha\). The rho windings of
102: \((\kappa,\alpha_L,g,\eta)\) are \((-6,0,0,0)\).
103: The actual periodic logarithms of every zero-winding factor are
104: retained.
105: 
106: Here is a precise connection choice that retains them. On the regular
107: boundary, use the actual real period coefficients
108: \[
109:  (z,\ell)=u(1,0)+v(\tau,\mu)+d(0,1)+\gamma(G,\beta),
110:  \qquad z=\frac{\log Z}{2\pi i},\quad
111:         \ell=\frac{\log V}{2\pi i},
112:                                                         \tag{EC.6}
113: \]
114: where
115: \(\tau=\log(\kappa q)/(2\pi i)\),
116: \(\mu=\log\alpha_L/(2\pi i)\),
117: \(G=\log g/(2\pi i)\) and
118: \(\beta=\log(\eta q)/(2\pi i)\).
119: The imaginary parts give exactly the invertible matrix in SQ.4a;
120: the real parts then determine \(u,d\). In particular
121: \[
122:  d=\operatorname{Re}\ell-\operatorname{Re}\mu\,v
123:                            -\operatorname{Re}\beta\,\gamma.
124:                                                         \tag{EC.7}
125: \]
126: Differentiating (EC.7) retains the differentials and values of both
127: unit logarithms. Thus using \(d\) as a coefficient coordinate does
128: not set their phases or their derivatives to zero.
129: 
130: The q and rho coordinate changes are respectively
